data.content:
Here are the TUnit equivalents, line by line:

```csharp
// Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);
Assert.That.IsEqualTo(actual, expected);

// Assert.IsTrue(order.IsValid);
Assert.That.IsTrue(order.IsValid);

// Assert.IsNull(result.Error);
Assert.That.IsNull(result.Error);

// Assert.IsInstanceOfType(handler, typeof(IOrderHandler));
Assert.That.IsTypeOf<IOrderHandler>(handler);

// CollectionAssert.Contains(processedIds, 42);
Assert.That.Contains(processedIds, 42);

// var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<InvalidOperationException>(() => processor.Run());
var ex = Assert.That.Throws<InvalidOperationException>(() => processor.Run());

// Assert.AreEqual("Order is empty", ex.Message);
Assert.That.IsEqualTo(ex.Message, "Order is empty");
```

**Key differences:** TUnit uses a fluent `Assert.That.*` pattern. `AreEqual(expected, actual)` becomes `IsEqualTo(actual, expected)` — note the parameter order swap. `IsInstanceOfType` becomes the generic `IsTypeOf<T>`, and `ThrowsException` becomes `Throws`.
